# Cloudflare Pages redirects. Two things about this file decide whether a rule
# actually fires, and both are counter-intuitive:
#
#  1. ORDER, NOT SPECIFICITY. "If there are multiple redirects for the same
#     source path, the top-most redirect is applied." A general wildcard placed
#     above a specific rule therefore SWALLOWS it. That is why the ai-jargon
#     alias below sits above /posts/*, not below it.
#  2. A SLASH-LESS PATH IS ITS OWN SOURCE. /x/* does not match /x, so every
#     moved path gets a bare rule as well as a wildcard. Since blog-priv#64 the
#     site DOES have a custom 404, so an uncaught /x now returns a real 404
#     rather than soft-404ing to the homepage. Keep the bare rules anyway: a 301
#     to the page the reader actually wanted beats even a correct 404, both for
#     them and for the ranking the old URL still carries.
#
# Every rule is 301 (permanent): 302 tells search engines the move is temporary
# and the old URL keeps the ranking. No rule's target is another rule's source
# (no redirect chains) - asserted by scripts/test_redirects_order.py.

# blog-priv#59: Consulting -> Hire Hoi URL migration. The old /consulting/* tree
# now lives at /hire-hoi/ai-consultancy/*. Explicit 301 (permanent) so search
# engines and externally cached share-cards follow the move.
# The bare /consulting (no trailing slash) needs its own rule: the wildcard below
# does not match a slash-less path. Without this rule it would now land on the
# custom 404 (blog-priv#64) rather than the homepage, which is more honest but
# still loses a reader who asked for a page that exists.
/consulting /hire-hoi/ai-consultancy/ 301
/consulting/* /hire-hoi/ai-consultancy/:splat 301

# blog-priv#62: "Blogs" became a real level in the information architecture, so
# the 79 posts and the 7 category landings moved under /blogs/. 87 retired URLs,
# each mapping 1:1 (scripts/test_redirects_coverage.py asserts every one of them
# resolves to a URL the new sitemap actually serves).
#
# These 39 files across four other repos link to the retired URLs - including a
# submitted funding application and material already sent to recruiters - and
# cannot be retroactively edited. The rules below are the only thing keeping
# those links alive.

# Two posts publish under a slug that is not their bundle name (see the same list in
# scripts/validate_internal_links.py): 2026-04-07-foundation -> foundation, and
# ai-jargon-for-noobs -> ai-jargon-for-newbies. Only the second needs a rule here, and
# the reason is its ALIAS, not its slug: it sets aliases: ["/posts/ai-jargon-for-noobs/"],
# so that URL really was served, and the /posts/* wildcard would send it to a dead
# /blogs/ai-jargon-for-noobs/. foundation never served under its bundle name, so the
# wildcard already maps its one real URL correctly. This MUST stay above that wildcard
# (see 1 above).
/posts/ai-jargon-for-noobs /blogs/ai-jargon-for-newbies/ 301
/posts/ai-jargon-for-noobs/ /blogs/ai-jargon-for-newbies/ 301

# Posts. The wildcard also carries the section feed (/posts/index.xml ->
# /blogs/index.xml), since :splat is the whole remainder of the path.
/posts /blogs/ 301
/posts/* /blogs/:splat 301

# The 7 category landings. Each wildcard also carries that category's RSS feed
# (/tech-ai/index.xml -> /blogs/tech-ai/index.xml). Category order matches
# config/_default/menus.toml.
/tech-ai /blogs/tech-ai/ 301
/tech-ai/* /blogs/tech-ai/:splat 301
/entrepreneurship /blogs/entrepreneurship/ 301
/entrepreneurship/* /blogs/entrepreneurship/:splat 301
/trading /blogs/trading/ 301
/trading/* /blogs/trading/:splat 301
/food-booze /blogs/food-booze/ 301
/food-booze/* /blogs/food-booze/:splat 301
/adventure /blogs/adventure/ 301
/adventure/* /blogs/adventure/:splat 301
/dance /blogs/dance/ 301
/dance/* /blogs/dance/:splat 301
/life /blogs/life/ 301
/life/* /blogs/life/:splat 301

# The `categories` taxonomy is switched off (blog-priv#62 Phase 6, hugo.toml
# [taxonomies]). Its 8 URLs listed exactly the same posts as the landings above,
# so each term maps onto its landing and the index maps onto the hub. `series` is
# deliberately untouched (A11) and has no rules here. `tags` had none either
# until blog-priv#66, which retired 7 mechanically-duplicate terms and added
# their 301s at the end of this file.
/categories /blogs/ 301
/categories/* /blogs/:splat 301

# blog-priv#66 Phase 4: mechanical tag merges (casing, plural and whitespace
# duplicates of a tag that already existed). Eight merges were applied to post
# front matter, but only these SIX retire a /tags/ URL, and the count was derived
# from source rather than read off the merge list:
#
#  * "Brazilian Zouk" -> zouk retires nothing. Its slug /tags/brazilian-zouk/ is
#    ALSO generated by what-is-zouk-aam, which carries the literal lowercase tag
#    `brazilian-zouk`. A rule here would 301 away a page that still builds.
#  * "AI" -> ai retires nothing either. Hugo already slugs both to `ai`, so the
#    rule would be the self-loop /tags/ai/ -> /tags/ai/. test_redirects_order.py
#    `check_no_chains` skips a rule that matches its own target, so nothing would
#    have caught it.
#
# Two lines per retired term, per rule 2 in the header: the wildcard does not
# match the slash-less form, and scripts/test_redirects_coverage.py asserts both
# forms. The wildcard drops :splat deliberately - a merged term has no children
# today (no tag paginates), and page 2 of the old term is not page 2 of the new
# one, so every deep path lands on the term page itself.
/tags/congress /tags/congresses/ 301
/tags/congress/* /tags/congresses/ 301
/tags/beginners /tags/beginner/ 301
/tags/beginners/* /tags/beginner/ 301
/tags/trading-system /tags/trading-systems/ 301
/tags/trading-system/* /tags/trading-systems/ 301
/tags/maturity-grade /tags/maturity-grading/ 301
/tags/maturity-grade/* /tags/maturity-grading/ 301
/tags/lessons-learned /tags/lessons/ 301
/tags/lessons-learned/* /tags/lessons/ 301
/tags/community-building /tags/community/ 301
/tags/community-building/* /tags/community/ 301
/tags/llms /tags/llm/ 301
/tags/llms/* /tags/llm/ 301
